# ⚠️ LF in the working tree, on every platform. GitHub's Windows runner has
# Git-for-Windows' core.autocrlf=true default, so without this a Windows
# checkout gets CRLF — and this project reads its OWN files with regexes that
# anchor on "\n". The contrast check extracts the page's colour tokens with
# /:root \{\n([\s\S]*?)\n  \}/, which matches nothing against "{\r\n": it
# reported every dark token and five of seven light ones as "not found in
# map.html", on CI only, while the page itself was perfectly fine.
#
# It matters more than one check. `bin/ccwatch` is read by bash, and a CRLF
# shebang line is a "bad interpreter" error with a carriage return invisibly
# in the middle of it; the embedded python and jq programs are parsed the
# same way. There is nothing here that wants CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never touches them whatever the heuristics decide.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
